ddr) and a corresponding DHCPOFFER generated for node3's MAC, confirming the server logic and relay path are both functioning on the server side.","evidence_ids":["cmd-22f7e365d31a4502","cmd-e85415a9895c453d","cmd-c9bc507fc29c4dd6","cmd-e74739f407a04d68","cmd-7c55baf173804c82","cmd-98a842bf9d374843","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"node3 actually obtains an IP address (end-to-end) from node2's DHCP service via the relay.","status":"not_satisfied","summary":"Despite the relay and server both functioning (DISCOVER relayed, OFFER generated), node3's client-side attempts to bring up enp8s0 via NetworkManager failed repeatedly and consistently across the entire ~19-minute observation window (07:59 through 08:17), each attempt ending in 'IP configuration could not be reserved (no available address, timeout, etc.)' or the connection state stalling at 'connecting (getting IP configuration)' without ever reaching 'connected'. Direct packet captures on node1's eth1/eth2 and on node2 during these attempts show only client DHCP Request broadcasts/relayed unicasts and never an OFFER or ACK packet traversing back to node3, and node3's own leases are never shown as bound. The final captured state for node3 shows enp8s0 disconnected or still negotiating, with no successful lease at any point in the trace.","evidence_ids":["cmd-08ab4595cb184448","cmd-7fe0a1f1e3394f60","cmd-b54c28ffda824a1a","cmd-a08ec1c306c64591","cmd-4933167de8dd414d","cmd-c25c29537e8b4ed7","cmd-de0a62757505460f","cmd-82114f4addab4df3","cmd-744623020c35435a","cmd-917ec8cb4d1a4c42","cmd-c899a69de2db4fc0","cmd-373ba0149e394d26","cmd-4fd5a6aabf054172","cmd-8982e97d4ba14775"]}],"confidence":0.75,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.5,"summary":"Most state-changing commands were directly tied to building or verifying the relay/server solution (static IP assignment on node1/node2, dhcp-server and isc-dhcp-relay installation/config, firewalld rule, service enablement) and are justified. However, the trace shows a long, unresolved troubleshooting loop: roughly a dozen repeated tcpdump capture sessions and a dozen repeated nmcli con up/down attempts on node3 over ~19 minutes without ever diagnosing the root cause, leaving numerous abandoned artifacts. The after-executor lifecycle snapshot shows many leftover temporary files never cleaned up on all three nodes (e.g. node1: /tmp/tcp*.log, /tmp/e1.log, /tmp/e2.log, /tmp/eth1.pcap, /tmp/eth2.pcap, /tmp/tcp*.pid; node2: /tmp/n2.out, /tmp/tmpiwlewv71; node3: /tmp/e3*.log, /tmp/e3c.pcap, /tmp/nm*.log) as residue from diagnostic commands. NetworkManager logging on node3 was also switched to DEBUG level for troubleshooting and never restored to its default INFO level (cmd-a8e5cfd143e246e7), a low-risk but unreverted configuration change. No destructive actions, security-control weakening (the firewalld dhcp rule is a required, scoped opening), or damaged package state were observed; failed-services lists remain unchanged (only pre-existing SONiC failures persist). Additionally, the mandated provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol was not executed for any node (no restart acknowledgements or reboot cycles appear anywhere in the trace), which is a protocol gap independent of task success.","evidence_ids":["global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files","cmd-a8e5cfd143e246e7","cmd-e74739f407a04d68","global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]},"overall_summary":"The executor correctly built the DHCP-relay topology: node1 was configured with per-segment addresses and isc-dhcp-relay pointed at node2, and node2 was configured with an ISC dhcpd instance that declares both the relay-facing subnet and node3's actual subnet with a usable address range, firewall-permitted and running. Server-side logs and packet captures confirm the relay mechanics work — node3's DHCPDISCOVER broadcasts are captured on its segment, relayed as unicast to node2, and node2 responds with a DHCPOFFER. However, the end result required by the task — node3 actually obtaining an address — was never achieved in any of the roughly fifteen repeated attempts spanning nearly 20 minutes; node3's client consistently timed out ('IP configuration could not be reserved') and its interface never reached a connected/leased state by the end of the captured trace. No OFFER/ACK packets were ever observed reaching node3 in the capture evidence, despite the server generating them. The executor's own final report is unavailable and the run appears to have been cancelled while still mid-troubleshooting, leaving the core objective unmet alongside some diagnostic residue (leftover temp/log files, an unreverted NetworkManager debug-logging change) and no restart-cycle evidence for any node.","limitations":["Executor final report was unavailable ('blocked', no actions/evidence supplied); the after_executor lifecycle snapshot itself reports a CancelledError, indicating the run was likely interrupted mid-troubleshooting rather than concluded.","No successful DHCP lease acquisition by node3 was observed at any point in the ~19-minute captured window, but the root cause of the repeated failures (e.g., firewall, broadcast/unicast handling, ARP, or relay binding) was not diagnosed in the trace, so it is unclear whether the issue is fixable with a small additional change or reflects a deeper misconfiguration.","The provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol (pre/post boot identity, restart acknowledgement, recovered state) was not performed for any of the three nodes in this trace; no reboot cycles appear anywhere in the command timeline, so restart/recovery behavior cannot be assessed.","The preparation phase was disabled/aliased to before_prepare, so no prepare-vs-executor separation was possible beyond the baseline itself; all changes are attributed to the executor by default."]},"ttft_